86
00:02:41,490 --> 00:02:42,500
It was to make the watch

87
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:43,720
easier to use.

88
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:47,870
Santos-Dumont had a friend with a

89
00:02:47,880 --> 00:02:49,030
different kind of workshop:

90
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:50,550
Louis Cartier.

91
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:53,590
Cartier's own history dates the answer

92
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:54,750
to nineteen oh-four,

93
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:57,350
when Louis created a wristwatch for

94
00:02:57,360 --> 00:02:59,050
the aviator so he could consult

95
00:02:59,060 --> 00:03:00,370
the time while flying.

96
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:03,010
The familiar story is wonderfully

97
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:03,730
simple.

98
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:05,590
The man working on the future

99
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,950
of transport brings a practical problem

100
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:09,610
to a man working on beautiful

101
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:12,550
objects. Their worlds meet at the

102
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:12,910
wrist.

103
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:15,470
There's no need to imagine an

104
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:17,030
